#!/bin/sh
# Work Item SOP -- ring 2. SOURCE OF RECORD; copies live in each declared
# public repository as tools/hooks/commit-msg.
#
# Ruled by DT2 (briefs/2026-09-12-work-item-reliability/08-DT2 §2), ratified
# "I ratify stages 2-4 as amended by 08-DT2 -- David Facer, 9/11/2026".
#
# STAGE IS PER SURFACE AND IS READ, NOT WRITTEN HERE. The fourth field of
# .work-item-surfaces -- work_item_enforced -- decides it for the surface this
# invocation is running on:
#
#   false or absent -> STAGE 3. Warns. Refuses nothing. Exits 0.
#   true            -> STAGE 4. Warns and REFUSES. Exits 1.
#
# THIS HOOK STATES ITS MODE ON EVERY WARNING IT PRINTS. A control whose output
# cannot be read against its behaviour is a control nobody can check.
#
# WHY THE MODE IS A FIELD AND NOT A FLAG IN THIS FILE. Stage 4 takes a SEPARATE
# SIGNATURE PER SURFACE, and this file is copied byte-identically into every
# declared repository -- so there is no line here that could say "enforced on
# two of eleven" without forking the copies. Making enforcement a generated
# corpus field keeps one hook, keeps the copies identical, and makes a
# per-surface signature what this practice already knows how to record: a
# signature on a corpus row. Ruled by DT2 in
# briefs/2026-09-16-reconstitution-run/27-DT2 section 1, on the RULE-9
# consultation requested in 26-CC.
#
# ENFORCED SINCE 2026-09-19 on two surfaces, signed "I enforce
# ai-native-ea-maturity-model and aimaturitymodels -- David Facer 9/19/2026",
# after the stage 4 ratification of the day before. WHICH surfaces are enforced
# is NOT listed here: that is the register's to say and this file's to read.
# A list here would be the derived-value-by-memory class, in a control.
#
# NO DATE APPEARS IN THIS HOOK'S OUTPUT AND NONE SHOULD. The warn period used
# to be printed as "2026-09-12 to 2026-09-17 UTC" -- a hand-maintained derived
# value inside a control, with a guaranteed expiry, and it did expire while
# still being printed. RULE-8. 27-DT2 section 4 ruled it out and ruled that it
# does not come back.
#
# ROLLBACK (27-DT2 section 6). Setting work_item_enforced back to false, or
# removing it, returns a surface to stage 3. That is available to David without
# a further consultation and introduces no governance -- it restores the state
# stage 3 already ratified, and it records itself as a corpus row change.
# TIGHTENING is structural and takes the consultation route. Removing stage 3
# as well -- a surface that neither warns nor refuses -- is a third thing and
# is authorised by nothing here.
#
# WHY commit-msg AND NOT pre-commit. pre-commit runs BEFORE the message exists
# and is handed nothing -- proven by attempting, 2026-09-12, both hooks
# instrumented in a throwaway repo: pre-commit argc=0, commit-msg receives
# .git/COMMIT_EDITMSG. 03-DT2 §3 originally placed ring 2 in pre-commit and was
# corrected before execution. This hook reads the message AND shells
# `git diff --cached` for the staged paths, so one hook has both halves.
#
# HOW THIS CONTROL CAN BE CROSSED (RULE-10)
#
# RE-DECLARED 2026-09-19, ruled by DT2 in 27-DT2 section 5. The four paths are
# unchanged; WHAT CROSSING THEM COSTS is not, and a declaration written for a
# hook that warned does not describe a hook that refuses.
#
#   1. `git commit --no-verify` skips it entirely. Git's documented behaviour,
#      identical to the pre-commit gate in the governed repo.
#      COST CHANGED: under stage 3 this skipped a warning -- a convenience.
#      Under stage 4 it BYPASSES A REFUSAL. Same flag, different act.
#   2. IT CANNOT SEE COMMITS THAT NEVER TOUCH A LOCAL GIT CLIENT. A GitHub
#      Action or API commit runs no local hook at all. Measured: open-wire-bot
#      authored 10 of open-wire-editions' 26 commits in 30 days as "Heartbeat".
#      Ring 2 covers workstation commits and nothing else, by construction --
#      which is why ring 3 exists and why it is not optional.
#      COST CHANGED: under stage 3 such a commit wrote no warn-log line. Under
#      stage 4 it is not reached at all, so an enforced surface receiving them
#      is enforced against one population and open to another. That is ring
#      3's, and 27-DT2 section 8 declines to carry it on OI-82.
#   3. A fresh clone has no hooks until `git config core.hooksPath tools/hooks`
#      is run. Per-clone, never committed. Same gap the governed repo's own
#      pre-commit header names and does not solve.
#      COST CHANGED, AND THIS IS THE ONE TO READ. Under stage 3 an unset
#      hooksPath meant a silent hook. Under stage 4 it means ENFORCEMENT IS
#      ABSENT AND NOTHING SAYS SO -- the clone reads as compliant rather than
#      as unguarded. Measured 2026-09-19: set in 11 of 11 clones on one machine
#      at one instant, which is a statement about a moment and not about the
#      estate. Declared, not solved.
#   4. .work-item-surfaces absent or unreadable -> this hook says nothing.
#      Fail-open, deliberately: a surface nobody declared is not governed
#      (01-DTOG section 2 P1), and a warn-mode hook that shouted about its own
#      missing config would train people to ignore it before it ever refuses.
#      COST CHANGED: fail-open now means REFUSES NOTHING, so a deleted or
#      corrupt projection silently disables stage 4 on that surface. The guard
#      is gen-surface-projection.js, which acquired a caller on 2026-09-19
#      (tools/hooks/pre-commit GATE 4, and corpus-validation.yml) precisely
#      because this became load-bearing.
#   5. A THREE-FIELD PROJECTION LEAVES THE FOURTH FIELD EMPTY, which reads as
#      not-enforced and warns. Deliberate and stated: it is the safe direction,
#      and it is why the field needed no flag day. But a surface whose deployed
#      projection was never updated is SIGNED AND NOT ENFORCED, and nothing on
#      the surface itself detects that. Added 2026-09-19 with the field.
#
# Authority for all five: this file, as the governed source of record. Each is
# reconsidered when the stage changes for any surface -- the event that
# rewrote costs 1-4 today.

MSG_FILE="$1"
[ -n "$MSG_FILE" ] && [ -f "$MSG_FILE" ] || exit 0

repo_root=$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null) || exit 0
SURFACES="$repo_root/.work-item-surfaces"
[ -f "$SURFACES" ] || exit 0

staged=$(git diff --cached --name-only 2>/dev/null)
[ -n "$staged" ] || exit 0

# Does this commit touch a declared in-scope surface, and is that surface
# ENFORCED?
# Projection format:
#   <repository>\t<path or ->\t<work_item_required>\t<work_item_enforced>
#
# CR STRIPPING, ADDED 2026-09-12, AND IT IS THE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN THIS HOOK
# WORKING AND DOING NOTHING. .work-item-surfaces is committed with LF, and a
# clone with core.autocrlf=true -- which every clone in this estate has --
# receives it as CRLF ON CHECKOUT. `read` then hands the LAST field a trailing
# carriage return, so `required` is "true\r", the test below is FALSE for every
# row, `touched` stays empty, and this hook exits 0 having printed nothing.
# Not an error. Silence.
#
# Which meant ring 2 fired ONLY in the clone that generated its own projection
# and had never re-checked it out. Found 2026-09-12 when a cherry-pick onto
# main gave superdtf-0882.github.io a fresh CRLF copy and a real commit to a
# declared surface passed unwarned -- inside OI-76's own warn window, which
# was therefore measuring a control that could not fire. Brief:
# briefs/2026-09-12-work-item-reliability/12-CC.
#
# All four fields are stripped, not just the last: a row with fewer fields
# than expected parks the CR on whichever field ends up last -- which is
# exactly what a three-field projection does to `required`, and is why every
# field is stripped rather than the one that is nominally last.
# ${var%$CR} is POSIX parameter expansion -- $'\r' is a bashism and this is
# #!/bin/sh. And the loop still redirects from the file rather than piping
# `tr -d '\r'` into it, because a pipe runs the loop in a subshell and
# `touched` would not survive it -- which would replace this bug with a
# quieter one.
#
# THE LOOP NO LONGER BREAKS ON THE FIRST MATCH, and that is a behaviour
# change rather than a tidy-up. With a per-surface mode, a repository whose
# projection holds several rows can have one enforced and another not, and
# breaking early would take the mode of whichever row happened to sort first.
# Every matching row is now read and THE STRICTER WINS -- the same rule the
# generator applies when two rows collapse onto one surface.
CR=$(printf '\r')
touched=""
enforce=""
while IFS="$(printf '\t')" read -r _repo spath required enforced; do
  _repo=${_repo%"$CR"}
  spath=${spath%"$CR"}
  required=${required%"$CR"}
  enforced=${enforced%"$CR"}
  case "$_repo" in \#*|"") continue ;; esac
  [ "$required" = "true" ] || continue
  hit=""
  if [ "$spath" = "-" ]; then
    hit="yes"
  elif printf '%s\n' "$staged" | grep -q "^$spath/"; then
    hit="yes"
  fi
  [ -n "$hit" ] || continue
  touched="yes"
  if [ "$enforced" = "true" ]; then
    enforce="yes"
  fi
done < "$SURFACES"

[ -n "$touched" ] || exit 0

# Trailer must be qualified: "OKF-TOGAF#119", never a bare "#119".
# 08-DT2 §3: the repository qualifier is mandatory even while it is constant.
if grep -Eq '^Work-Item:[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+#[0-9]+[[:space:]]*$' "$MSG_FILE"; then
  exit 0
fi

# THE MODE IS STATED ON EVERY WARNING, ruled by DT2 in 27-DT2 section 4.
# It is read from the projection for THIS surface, never written here, and
# it carries no date: the old text named a warn period that had already
# expired while the hook went on printing it.
if [ -n "$enforce" ]; then
  wi_headline="WORK ITEM SOP -- REFUSED. This commit touches an ENFORCED public surface and carries no Work-Item trailer."
  wi_mode_a="  MODE: STAGE 4 -- REFUSING on this surface. This commit was NOT created."
  wi_mode_b="  Enforcement is declared per surface in corpus/catalog_applications.md"
  wi_mode_c="  (work_item_enforced) and reaches this hook through the projection below."
else
  wi_headline="WORK ITEM SOP -- this commit touches a declared public surface and carries no Work-Item trailer."
  wi_mode_a="  MODE: STAGE 3 -- WARNING on this surface. Nothing is refused."
  wi_mode_b="  Enforcement is declared per surface in corpus/catalog_applications.md"
  wi_mode_c="  (work_item_enforced) and is not set for this one."
fi

echo "" >&2
echo "$wi_headline" >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "$wi_mode_a" >&2
echo "$wi_mode_b" >&2
echo "$wi_mode_c" >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "  Add a trailer to the commit message, qualified:" >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "      Work-Item: OKF-TOGAF#NNN" >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "  No Work Item yet? Create one from the governed repo:" >&2
echo "      node tools/type-issue.js create --repo superdtf-0882/OKF-TOGAF \\" >&2
echo "        --title \"...\" --body-file <path> --type work_item \\" >&2
echo "        --realizes INT-002 --strata S6" >&2
echo "" >&2
echo "  Declared surfaces for this repo: $SURFACES" >&2
echo "  Governing record: corpus/catalog_applications.md (OKF-TOGAF)" >&2
echo "" >&2

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE WARN LOG. Ruled by DT2 2026-09-12 (briefs/2026-09-12-work-item-
# reliability/13-DT2 §2) and PROMOTED FROM RIDER TO GATE: OI-76's second
# warn window does not open until this line exists. The first window was
# closed as ABORTED because its instrument could not fire, and DTOG's
# standing question -- are warnings retained anywhere? -- had assumed a
# numerator the window never had. A second window without a log would be
# the same instrument-less count with a fresh date.
#
# WHAT THIS MEASURES (RULE-4 scope). One line per occasion on which this
# hook PRINTED the warning above. That is all it is. It does NOT measure:
# commits made without a trailer (a --no-verify commit never reaches
# here), whether any human read the warning, or whether the missing
# trailer was later added by amend. The denominator for "how often does a
# public-surface commit lack a trailer" is NOT in this file and cannot be
# derived from it.
#
# A FOURTH FIELD WAS ADDED 2026-09-19: WARNED or REFUSED. Until stage 4 a
# line meant one thing, so the mode did not need recording; now the same
# log holds both outcomes and a count that did not distinguish them would
# be the coverage-rate mistake again. It is APPENDED rather than inserted,
# and nothing parses this file positionally -- grepped across tools/ and
# .github/ on 2026-09-19 and found no reader at all; it has been read by
# hand every time. A REFUSED line records a commit that DOES NOT EXIST,
# which is the one way this log now differs in kind from what it was.
#
# HOW THIS CONTROL CAN BE CROSSED (RULE-10)
#   1. IT LIVES IN .git/ AND DOES NOT TRAVEL. Never committed, never
#      pushed, per-clone exactly as core.hooksPath is. A warning in one
#      clone is invisible in every other, and a fresh clone starts empty.
#      Counting a window means collecting from each clone by hand.
#   2. `git commit --no-verify` skips the hook, so it skips the log. The
#      commit that most wants counting is the one that can opt out.
#   3. A commit that never touches a local git client -- a GitHub Action,
#      the API, the web editor -- runs no hook and writes no line.
#   4. The file is append-only by convention, not by permission. Anyone
#      with the clone can edit or delete it, and nothing detects that.
#   5. If .git/ is unwritable the append fails and IS DELIBERATELY
#      SWALLOWED. A logging failure must never break a commit, and a
#      warn-mode hook that shouted about its own logging would train
#      people to ignore it before stage 4 ever refuses.
LOG="$repo_root/.git/work-item-warnings.log"
# Each field is built and bounded to ONE LINE before printf, because the
# obvious form -- $(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD || echo '?') -- emits
# BOTH the command's output and the fallback when the command prints
# something AND fails, which is exactly what rev-parse does in a
# repository with no commits yet: "HEAD" on stdout, non-zero exit. That
# wrote two log lines per warning and was caught by the harness counting
# lines rather than by reading the code.
log_when=$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ 2>/dev/null)
log_branch=$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD 2>/dev/null | head -n 1)
[ -n "$log_branch" ] || log_branch="?"
log_files=$(printf '%s' "$staged" | tr '\n' ',')
if [ -n "$enforce" ]; then log_mode="REFUSED"; else log_mode="WARNED"; fi
printf '%s\t%s\t%s\t%s\n' "$log_when" "$log_branch" "$log_files" "$log_mode" >> "$LOG" 2>/dev/null || true

# STAGE 3 WARNS; STAGE 4 REFUSES. WHICH ONE IS NOT DECIDED HERE -- it came
# from work_item_enforced in the projection, for the surface this commit
# touches. See the header before changing either line. The `exit 0` is still
# load-bearing governance for every surface that is not enforced, and it is
# still the majority of them.
if [ -n "$enforce" ]; then
  exit 1
fi
exit 0
